  return (str.match(/\S+/g) || []).length;
}
```

Here `match` returns `null` when there are no matches, so `|| []` ensures the result is `0` instead of an error.

**Note:** Both versions treat punctuation attached to words as part of the word (e.g., `"Hello, world!"` counts as 2 words). Standalone punctuation, like the dash in `"wait - what"`, counts as a word. If you need stricter behavior, you could match only letters and numbers:

```javascript
