    throw new RangeError("Input must be a non-negative integer.");
  }

  let result = 1;

  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    result *= i;
  }

  return result;
}

console.log(factorial(0)); // 1
console.log(factorial(5)); // 120
```

This uses an iterative approach, avoiding recursion overhead. Note that JavaScript’s `Number` type cannot precisely represent very large factorials; use `BigInt` if you need results beyond `170!`.

