async function fetchJSON(url) {
  const response = await fetch(url);
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP error ${response.status}: ${response.statusText}`);
  }
  return response.json();
}
```

Usage:

```javascript
try {
  const data = await fetchJSON('https://api.example.com/data');
  console.log(data);
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Failed to fetch JSON:', err);
}
```

Notes:
- `response.json()` returns a promise, so `return`ing it inside an `async` function resolves it automatically.
- The `response.ok` check throws on 4xx/5xx responses, since `fetch` only rejects on network errors, not HTTP errors.
- If you want to pass options (headers, method, etc.), add a second parameter: `async function fetchJSON(url, options) { ... fetch(url, options) ... }`.

```javascript
async function fetchJSON(url) {
  const response = await fetch(url);

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Request failed with status ${response.status}`);
  }

  return response.json();
}
```

Usage:

```javascript
try {
  const data = await fetchJSON('https://api.example.com/data');
  console.log(data);
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Failed to fetch JSON:', err);
}
```

A few notes on the implementation:

- **`async`/`await`** keeps the call site readable — callers can just `await fetchJSON(...)` instead of chaining `.then()`.
- **Checking `response.ok`** is important: `fetch` only rejects on network errors, not on HTTP errors like 404 or 500. Without this check, you'd try to parse an error page as JSON.
- **`response.json()`** returns a promise that resolves to the parsed body — returning it directly lets the caller's `await` (or `.then`) handle it.
- If you want to pass options (headers, method, body), extend the signature:

```javascript
async function fetchJSON(url, options = {}) {
  const response = await fetch(url, options);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
  return response.json();
}
```

# Fetch and Parse JSON

```javascript
async function fetchJSON(url) {
  const response = await fetch(url);
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP error ${response.status}: ${response.statusText}`);
  }
  return response.json();
}
```

## Usage

```javascript
try {
  const data = await fetchJSON('https://api.example.com/users');
  console.log(data);
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Failed to fetch JSON:', err);
}
```

## Notes

- **`async`/`await`** — the function returns a `Promise` that resolves to the parsed object/array.
- **`response.ok`** — `fetch` only rejects on network failures, not on HTTP errors like `404` or `500`, so we check `ok` (status in the 200–299 range) explicitly.
- **`response.json()`** — parses the body as JSON and also returns a promise, which we return directly.
- If you want to add extras like timeouts, custom headers, or `AbortController` support, wrap the `fetch` call in a `try/catch` and pass options through:

```javascript
async function fetchJSON(url, options = {}) {
  const response = await fetch(url, options);
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP error ${response.status}: ${response.statusText}`);
  }
  return response.json();
}
```

# Fetch JSON Function

```javascript
/**
 * Fetches a URL and returns the parsed JSON response.
 * @param {string} url - The URL to fetch.
 * @param {RequestInit} [options] - Optional fetch options (method, headers, body, etc.).
 * @returns {Promise<any>} The parsed JSON body.
 */
async function fetchJSON(url, options = {}) {
  const response = await fetch(url, options);

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status} ${response.statusText} for ${url}`);
  }

  return response.json();
}
```

## Usage

```javascript
// Simple GET
const data = await fetchJSON('https://api.example.com/users');
console.log(data);

// POST with a JSON body
const created = await fetchJSON('https://api.example.com/users', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Ada' }),
});

// With error handling
try {
  const data = await fetchJSON('https://api.example.com/missing');
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Request failed:', err.message);
}
```

## Notes

- **`response.ok` check is important.** `fetch` only rejects on network failures, not on HTTP error statuses like `404` or `500`. Without this check, you'd try to parse an error page as JSON.
