This uses the built-in `fetch` API, checks that the request succeeded, and returns the parsed JSON. If the response has a non-2xx status or the body isn't valid JSON, it throws an error.

```js
/**
 * Fetch a URL and return the parsed JSON response.
 *
 * @param {string} url - The URL to request.
 * @param {object} [options] - Optional fetch options (headers, method, body, etc.).
 * @returns {Promise<any>} The parsed JSON payload.
 * @throws {Error} If the request fails or the response is not valid JSON.
 */
export async function fetchJson(url, options = {}) {
  const response = await fetch(url, {
    headers: { Accept: 'application/json', ...options.headers },
    ...options,
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(
      `HTTP ${response.status} ${response.statusText} for ${url}`
    );
  }

  // Guard against empty bodies (e.g. 204 No Content) causing a JSON parse error.
  const text = await response.text();
  if (!text) return null;

  try {
    return JSON.parse(text);
  } catch (err) {
    throw new Error(`Failed to parse JSON from ${url}: ${err.message}`);
  }
}
```

**Usage:**

```js
const data = await fetchJson('https://api.example.com/items');
console.log(data.items.length);

const created = await fetchJson('https://api.example.com/items', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Widget' }),
});
```

**Notes:**
- `response.ok` is checked before parsing so HTML error pages (common with 404/500) don't produce confusing `JSON.parse` errors.
- The body is read as text first so an empty response can be handled gracefully and parse failures can report the offending URL.
- `options.headers` is merged so caller-supplied headers win, while `Accept: application/json` is added by default.
- In Node.js, this requires Node 18+ (or the `node-fetch` polyfill); in browsers it works out of the box, though it will be subject to CORS restrictions.
